system.out: ELF 64-bit LSB pie executable, x86-64, version 1 (SYSV), dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, BuildID[sha1]=63224e5a94fa31cb071c82105d8a70ffc806ac0b, for GNU/Linux 3.2.0, not stripped
ELF means Executable and Linkable format
pie executable: Position Independent Executable. It’s built so the loader can place it at a random base address each run (ASLR). In a CTF, this means function addresses change between runs unless you leak one first.
x86-64: the target CPU is AMD64/Intel 64.
version 1 (SYSV): the ELF version and the ABI (System V), which is the normal Linux one.
dynamically linked: it uses shared libraries such as libc at runtime instead of bundling them in. So functions like printf and system are resolved through the PLT/GOT.
interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2: the dynamic loader that starts the program and loads those shared libraries.
BuildID[sha1]=…: a unique hash identifying this exact build. It’s used for matching debug symbols.
for GNU/Linux 3.2.0: the minimum kernel version it’s declared to run on.
not stripped: the symbol table is still in the binary. Function names like main and any custom functions are visible. This makes reversing much easier in tools like Ghidra, objdump -d, or gdb.

